{{Quote|I didn't hit it that hard. Must've had a self-destruct.|[[Han Solo]], after witnessing a Viper [[probe droid]] self-destruct|<ref name="ESB">{{Film|V}}</ref>}}
[[File:AntiCaptureProtocols-SWL99.png|thumb|300px|IG-11 initiated his self-destruct mechanism to save Grogu.]]
A '''self-destruct mechanism''' was a defensive system installed or embedded in some craft, such as [[starfighter]]s, [[starship]]s (like [[Imperial-class Star Destroyer|Imperial Star Destroyers]]), or simply [[probe droid]]s, that allowed it to destroy itself in certain circumstances akin to being under threat of capture or an attempt to protect information it was carrying. This was installed in [[Galactic Empire|Imperial]] [[Viper probe droid|probe droids]], which allowed them to self-destruct if confronted after they accomplished their tasks.<ref name="ESB" />

During the [[Clone Wars]], the [[Infiltrator demolition droid]]s are both the [[demolition droid]]s and have the explosion charges which means there are [[bomb]]ers of the [[Confederacy of Independent Systems]] set themselves to self-destruct to [[Bombing of Coruscant's central power distribution grid|blow up]] the [[central power distribution grid]] on [[Coruscant]] goes the [[Coruscant mandatory blackout|mandatory blackout]], even to stop the vote for the [[Republic financial reform bill|bill]] to de-regulate banking conglomerates.<ref name="Heroes on Both Sides">{{TCW|Heroes on Both Sides}}</ref>

In case their [[Star Destroyer]] were taken over by enemies, [[Imperial officer]]s were [[Destroyer-Level Craft Operations|taught]] three methods of self-destruct. The "automatic self-destruct" could only be set by combining the codes given to the three top [[officer]]s on board. It gave the longest time to detonation, allowing more [[Trooper|troops]] to reach the [[escape pod]]s in time. However, if the ship had been thoroughly infiltrated by enemy fighters, there was no guarantee that the three top officers would all be alive to use their codes, and the extra time to detonation would also give the invaders a greater chance to escape. The "core engine method" required to have access to the ship's [[reactor]], which could prove difficult during intra-ship combat. The "[[captain]]'s-word method" required that the captain signal for all personnel to abandon ship before sealing himself or herself in the [[Bridge (starship)|bridge]], providing cover for escape pods, and then [[pilot]]ing the vessel into the nearest [[planet]], [[star]], or other [[Gravity|gravitational]] singularity.<ref name="Lost Stars">''[[Lost Stars]]''</ref>

[[Grysk]]s preferred to use self-destruct mechanisms when the enemy could capture the ship and study it, and of course, know what species Grysk took control of. When [[Grand Admiral]] [[Mitth'raw'nuruodo|Thrawn]] and [[Admiral]] [[Ar'alani]] were fighting against the [[Grysk Hegemony]], specifically with a [[Unidentified Grysk commander (Grysk task force)|Grysk commander]], the Grysk destroyed his two [[warship]]s before the ''[[Steadfast (Chiss heavy cruiser)|Steadfast]]'' and the Imperial [[TIE/d "Defender" Multi-Role Starfighter|TIE defenders]] could capture the ship.<ref name="Treason">''[[Thrawn: Treason]]''</ref> [[Jixtus's fleet]], a [[fleet]] part of the Grysk military, self-destructed following the [[Kilji war cruiser]] ''[[Whetstone]]''{{'s}} [[Senior Captain Thrawn's last stand|attack]] on its [[flagship]], which also self-destructed.<ref name="LE">''[[Thrawn Ascendancy: Lesser Evil]]''</ref>

The [[E-XD-series infiltrator droid]]s have the self-destruct mechanisms that is called the "[[proton warhead]]" one is [[EXD-9]] that it blew up [[Imperial I-class Star Destroyer|''Imperial I''-class Star Destroyer]].<ref name="Warhead">{{Rebels|Warhead (episode)}}</ref>

